---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/code-block.astro';
import GuidePage from '../../layouts/guide-page.astro';
---

<GuidePage
  title="Tailwind"
  lead="Tailwind v4 with Nib's tokens as its theme: nib-* colours, type and spacing, and no radius or shadow scale."
>
  <h2 id="entry">The entry stylesheet</h2>
  <p>
    Name the layers first: Tailwind's theme and preflight, then Nib, then Tailwind's components and utilities. Nib's
    reset follows preflight, and a utility class still wins over a Nib component.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label="app.css"
    code={`@layer theme, base, nib, components, utilities;
@import 'tailwindcss';
@import '@nib-ui/tokens/tailwind.css';
@import '@nib-ui/css/nib.css';`}
  />
  <p>
    <code>nib init --css tailwind</code> writes exactly this file.
  </p>

  <h2 id="classes">Classes</h2>
  <p>
    Colours are <code>bg-nib-surface</code>, <code>text-nib-on-surface-2</code>, <code>border-nib-line</code>,
    <code>text-nib-red</code> and so on; type roles are <code>text-nib-label-md</code> and their siblings. The radius
    and shadow scales are reset, so <code>rounded-lg</code> and <code>shadow-md</code> draw nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Arbitrary values bypass the tokens (<code>rounded-[8px]</code>, <code>bg-[#f00]</code>) and Tailwind's own palette
    is still there (<code>text-red-500</code>). <code>nib lint</code> finds both in your markup.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label="card.html"
    code={`<article class="nib-tile">
  <h3 class="nib-tile__title">Kenya Nyeri</h3>
  <p class="text-nib-on-surface-2">Bright, blackcurrant, 1.5 kg left.</p>
</article>`}
  />
</GuidePage>
